<article class="env-proposition" id="06QJ">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06QJ">Proposition <span data-tag="06QJ">3.5.7.7</span>.</a> Let $n$ be an integer and let $i: Y \rightarrow X$ be a morphism of Kan complexes which admits a left homotopy inverse $r: X \rightarrow Y$. If $X$ is $n$-truncated, then $Y$ is $n$-truncated. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Fix a nonnegative integer $m \geq n+2$; we wish to show that every morphism $\sigma _0: \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{m} \rightarrow Y$ can be extended to an $m$-simplex of $Y$. By virtue of Variant <a data-tag="050W" href="/tag/050W">3.2.4.12</a>, this is equivalent to the assertion that $\sigma _0$ is nullhomotopic. We may therefore replace $\sigma _0$ by the homotopic map $r \circ i \circ \sigma _0$, and thereby reduce to the case where $\sigma _0 = r \circ \tau _0$ for some map $\tau _0: \operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{m} \rightarrow X$. In this case, it will suffice to show that $\tau _0$ can be extended to an $m$-simplex of $X$, which follows from our assumption that $X$ is $n$-truncated. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
